1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,047
Plenty of administration tasks mean working as a different user, or as root.

2
00:00:05,067 --> 00:00:08,487
Do it wrong, and files end up owned by the wrong person.

3
00:00:08,957 --> 00:00:13,374
So in this video: log in, and switch users, in multi-user targets.

4
00:00:17,394 --> 00:00:19,977
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

5
00:00:20,447 --> 00:00:23,627
Log in and switch users in multi-user targets.

6
00:00:24,097 --> 00:00:27,904
We'll check the target, switch users with su and sudo,

7
00:00:27,924 --> 00:00:30,455
and log in as another user over SSH.

8
00:00:37,975 --> 00:00:42,968
First, where are we? whoami says student. id shows our user ID and our groups.

9
00:00:45,938 --> 00:00:49,325
systemctl get-default says multi-user dot target.

10
00:00:49,345 --> 00:00:53,565
A target is a group of units that systemd brings up together.

11
00:00:53,585 --> 00:00:58,431
This one is a full multi-user system, with networking and text logins,

12
00:00:58,451 --> 00:01:00,169
but no graphical desktop.

13
00:01:09,639 --> 00:01:12,542
And it's active, so the system is running in it right now.

14
00:01:17,062 --> 00:01:19,890
To switch to someone, we need a second user.

15
00:01:19,910 --> 00:01:24,551
The lab script created one called alice, and gave her student's SSH key.

16
00:01:29,021 --> 00:01:32,122
That's lab preparation, not part of this objective.

17
00:01:32,142 --> 00:01:34,569
Creating users gets its own video later.

18
00:01:40,089 --> 00:01:45,249
su switches user. Without a dash, su alice keeps your current directory.

19
00:01:45,269 --> 00:01:50,501
We started in slash tmp, we are alice now, and we are still in slash tmp.

20
00:02:00,971 --> 00:02:04,200
With a dash, su dash alice starts a login shell:

21
00:02:04,220 --> 00:02:10,359
alice's home directory and alice's environment, as if she had just logged in. Use the dash.

22
00:02:19,829 --> 00:02:22,322
Dash c runs one command and returns.

23
00:02:22,342 --> 00:02:26,301
In a normal interactive shell you'd type exit to go back.

24
00:02:26,321 --> 00:02:29,931
Run by a regular user, su asks for alice's password.

25
00:02:29,951 --> 00:02:33,910
Our lab runner can't type one, so we ran it through sudo.

26
00:02:43,430 --> 00:02:48,867
sudo dash u runs one command as another user. Here it runs id as alice.

27
00:02:48,887 --> 00:02:54,247
sudo dash i gives root's login environment, and id shows user ID zero.

28
00:03:02,717 --> 00:03:04,354
sudo whoami returns root.

29
00:03:04,374 --> 00:03:09,259
sudo checks its rules and normally asks for your own password, not root's.

30
00:03:09,279 --> 00:03:12,639
This lab allows sudo without one, so it didn't ask.

31
00:03:25,159 --> 00:03:28,382
Now a real login: SSH to alice at localhost.

32
00:03:28,402 --> 00:03:31,624
We use a key, so there's no password prompt.

33
00:03:38,094 --> 00:03:44,052
It runs whoami, hostname, and pwd. We're alice, on node1, in alice's home.

34
00:03:44,072 --> 00:03:47,525
Every SSH login starts a fresh login shell.

35
00:03:59,045 --> 00:04:02,481
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

36
00:04:02,951 --> 00:04:06,456
Accounts and files live on disk, so they survive a reboot.

37
00:04:06,476 --> 00:04:08,947
We restart the lab, and it comes back up.

38
00:04:12,417 --> 00:04:15,884
Uptime says zero minutes, so this really is a fresh boot.

39
00:04:15,904 --> 00:04:18,882
The checker later confirms alice's work survived.

40
00:04:24,402 --> 00:04:27,620
Your turn. Switch to alice with a login shell,

41
00:04:27,640 --> 00:04:32,617
and create an empty file called lab2 dot txt in alice's home directory.

42
00:04:35,587 --> 00:04:36,983
Check that alice owns it.

43
00:04:37,003 --> 00:04:40,324
Then run the checker, and try before you open the solution.

44
00:04:46,344 --> 00:04:51,577
Next up: Locate, read, and use system documentation, including man, info,

45
00:04:51,597 --> 00:04:54,815
and files in slash usr slash share slash doc.

46
00:04:55,285 --> 00:04:58,187
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
